# Benchmarks and measurements

Correctness comes first. A performance claim comes with a measurement in the same PR.

## Tools

- Allocation: measuring code must not allocate itself. `GC.KeepAlive(object)` on an `int` boxes it (24 bytes per call in debug builds, optimized away in release); keep a result observable with a static sink field instead (S2).
- Allocation under JIT: a test project that asserts zero allocation runs with `<TieredCompilation>false</TieredCompilation>`. With tiering on, the macOS runner reported a one-off 248 to 7,112 bytes on the measuring thread in 2 of 3 runs, in a window with no JIT on that thread; with tiering off, 0 in every window (S6a, `tests/XylemUI.Core.Tests`). To find such a figure, split the measurement into windows and log the bytes with `JitInfo.GetCompiledMethodCount(currentThread: true)` for each (`SteadyStateAllocation` in `tests/XylemUI.Core.Tests` does). Tiering off did not end it: from L1a on, the runners still report a one-off 40 to 7,640 bytes in about 1 run in 6, in one window, with no method compiled and no collection on that thread (PR #91). Row Q1 (#92) found the cause with checkpoints in the layout pass: a `Return` of `ArrayPool<FlexItem>.Shared` allocated now and then on the runners, and flex scratch now comes from a per-thread stack (PR #102); after it, 10 runs of the CI in a row passed. One run in 35 of the experiments still counted 2,784 bytes between two child layouts, which no code there allocates. It is not explained, and with about 1 run in 35 failing, 10 runs in a row pass about 3 times in 4, so they do not show it is gone; row Q1 stays open until it is explained (the owner's decision on PR #102). A measuring helper does not call the measured code for the first time inside a window: PR #93's `SteadyStateAllocation` first called the iteration delegate in window 0 and measured its compilation (about 7 KB on ubuntu, PR #96). Check a new way of measuring against what it counts by itself, beside an allocation put in on purpose. When such a figure is chased on the runners, change one condition a run (PR #96 separated tests run one at a time from the unmeasured window, and so told the helper's own bytes apart). How many passes show that such a figure is gone follows from the rate it showed: with 1 failure in 35 left, 10 passes in a row come about 3 times in 4 (PR #102's review). A commit names as the cause only what was measured: PR #102 first wrote that a contended lock allocated, where the checkpoints showed only that one `Return` counted the bytes. Before measuring what a cache saves, count its hits: L4's kept base sizes, in one slot, were overwritten by the measuring and the layout run of the same pass and never hit, and every mutant of their key survived (PR #103). A commit that fixes such a figure names a cause only after the figure stopped in reruns (the bar of row Q1: 10 runs in a row). In L1b ef37b38 named a contended lock in ArrayPool's shared stacks as the cause; reruns still failed (the measuring helper's first call of its delegate was among the causes), and it was undone; the checkpoints later found the bytes in `Return`. Until row Q1 explains that one run (the owner's decisions on PR #91 and PR #102, temporary): a Zero-Alloc test that fails on a runner is run again; if the run passes, the PR may merge, and the failure's window and checkpoint figures go on the PR and on #92. A test that fails again on the run after is an allocation and blocks the merge.
- Allocation: the Zero-Alloc tests (`PerformanceAssert.DoesNotAllocate` in `XylemUI.Testing`, design §11.3) measure `GC.GetAllocatedBytesForCurrentThread()` after a warm-up, in the five scenarios the design names. They run in `./build.ps1` and are a gate. An allowed count stays or goes down; raising it needs an Issue.
- Micro-benchmarks: BenchmarkDotNet with `[MemoryDiagnoser]` in `benchmarks/XylemUI.Benchmarks`. Not a gate; CI does not run them on shared runners.
- End-to-end: `benchmarks/XylemUI.E2E` (10k rows, typing latency proxy, jank rate, startup) on a NativeAOT Release build. Recorded, not a gate (design §11.3).
- Profiles: `.agents/skills/cpu-profile/SKILL.md`.
- Framework comparison: `benchmarks/compare/` and `XylemUI.E2E external`, method and figures in `docs/benchmarks/m1-baseline.md`. How the outside tools fail on this setup (M1a):
  - PresentMon 2.6.0 without elevation misses the first presents of a process it learns of late, so it does not measure startup (Desktop Duplication does). Started before the app, it records nothing of the app; start the app first and pass `--process_id`. It writes stderr in UTF-16.
  - The frames of a covered or minimized window are not displayed, so its present-to-display time means nothing. `measure.ps1` minimizes every other window, and `ScreenWatch.BringToFront` makes the app's window topmost.
  - Avalonia's default composition does not appear in PresentMon; its frames are measured with `--redirection`.

## Rules

- Performance acceptance is judged on a NativeAOT Release build. JIT numbers include tiering and are only for finding where time goes.
- Profile before a speed row, and write on the Issue where the time goes. Order the work by the profile, not by the roadmap number.
- To find where the time of a path goes, cut it short: skip a stage, time what is left, take the difference. The stage that stands out is the one to work on.
- Compare both sides with the same configuration, data, and machine state, interleaved, three rounds or more, best of each side. Stop leftover processes from earlier runs. A difference inside the spread is not a result; "spread" is the statistics of repeated runs, not a guess.
- This machine's CPU (Core i5-13400F) has performance and efficiency cores. Cycles and time are measured with the process pinned to one performance core (logical processor 2) at high priority, as `benchmarks/counters/cycles.ps1` and `instructions.ps1` do with `-Processor`. Unpinned, the cycles of one relayout moved by about ±5 % and could not decide a 2 % difference; pinned, about ±3 % (L8).
- Equal instruction counts alone do not make a difference "the machine's conditions". Instructions do not see the waits for caches, branches, or memory. In L8 the instructions of L3 were within ±0.2 % and the PR said the time difference came from the machine; the review objected, and the pinned cycles showed +1.3 to 1.8 % in two ways of measuring (PR #151, row L10). Read the pinned cycles too, and count the stall events (`instructions.ps1 -Events`) for a difference the instructions do not explain.
- A row that names scenes or figures measures each of them, or says which it did not measure. The L8 PR closed the slower fixed-row change of L2 without counting it; the review found it (PR #151).
- Two builds compared run in a balanced order (A, B, B, A, repeated), so that each side runs first as often as second. In L5 sets that only alternated (A, B) showed a 7 % slower first layout for whichever build ran first; a code comment took one such set as the cause, and the next set undid it (PR #112).
- A baseline built from `main` in a worktree gets a copy of `vendor/out` (the native libraries are not tracked), and the worktree sits on a short path: one in the scratchpad could not be removed (`Filename too long`, L5).
- A call shorter than the clock's resolution is repeated inside one sample until the sample is long enough.
- A cost on the frame path is also measured inside a real frame: skip the stage in the sample and take the difference. A tight loop warms the caches; in S8b shaping and drawing one line took 1.35 µs per call in a loop of 1,000 but about 15 µs as the first call of a frame (a second line in the same frame added 2 to 6 µs). Report both, and say which one a budget is compared with.
- A speed PR reports time for the inputs the Issue names, next to a named baseline, and measures the paths it did not change. A change to code several subsystems share is measured on each of them.
- A figure that moved is explained from the rows, not from a guess (`.cursor/rules/open-ends.mdc`).
- A target names what it measures before it gets a number, and every side is measured with that definition. In M1a the startup target (250 ms) was set from the window's content (247 ms) while the screen showed the frame at 254 to 308 ms, and the GPU time read 0.07 ms from timestamp queries and 0.7 ms from PresentMon's `MsGPUBusy`; a review found both. The design now defines each (design §11.3).
- Latency from present to display is compared at the same frame period. Flutter presents every 46 ms on the measuring machine, so XylemUI was measured at 46 ms too (`app --period 46`, M1a).
- A change that makes the default path heavier (more memory, more work in a frame) measures again, before the PR, the figures of design §11.3 it moves: idle private memory, startup, frame times. T2 made ClearType the default and did not measure idle; a 1024 px RGBA atlas page for one line of text added 9.5 MB, past the 64 MB target, and the review found it (PR #183; ClearType pages became 512 px).
- A measurement that watches the screen (`startup`, `idle`, `text-capture`) runs alone, with no other process showing windows. In T2 an `idle` child waited for its marker while another measurement's windows covered it, and never ended (PR #183).
- The comparison apps are built with `benchmarks/compare/build.ps1` for the comparison, not taken from an earlier build in the main checkout. In T2 the Flutter build there was older than its `--text` scene and drew the M1 scene (PR #183).
- A run without a result is counted and reported (`startup_screen_missed`), not dropped. Dropping the runs that time out makes the median faster than the app is (M1a review).
- A harness that launches a process ends it on every path, a throw included. A leftover app from one run changes the next (M1a review).
- A comparison measures each framework at the version it ships with, and all three (Flutter, Avalonia, GPUI) before a reference is chosen. In L1a the agent measured Taffy's `main` for GPUI, whose crate then pinned Taffy 0.9.0 (2 to 5 times slower; Zed ships 0.13.0, row L9), and nearly took Taffy alone as the reference; the owner asked whether it was fast at all, and Flutter turned out fastest on the first layout and a change, Avalonia on a width change (`docs/benchmarks/l1a-layout.md`).
- Before a comparison, check each framework's latest stable release in a primary source (Flutter's `releases_windows.json`, NuGet, crates.io and the tag of the product's latest release) and build with it; the comparison build writes the versions it used (`versions.json`), and a table names them. The Flutter app is built with the SDK on PATH, which nothing pins: from L1a to L7 it stayed at 3.38.5 while 3.47.7 was out, and the owner asked which version was compared (PR #138).
- Every app of a comparison times at the same resolution. Flutter's `Stopwatch.elapsedMicroseconds` is a whole number, so a relayout near 2 µs read as 2.0 in every round; from `elapsedTicks` and `frequency` it was 2.7 µs (L7, PR #138).
- A scene added to the comparison goes into every comparison app in the same PR: Flutter, Avalonia, and Taffy for GPUI. In L2 the absolute scene went to Flutter and Avalonia only, and the owner asked where Taffy was (PR #125).
- Another framework's layout is measured through its layout manager. An Avalonia control measured outside a window has none, so a change of a child never reached its parent and the relayout read 0.1 µs (L1a); a headless window's `UpdateLayout` measured it.
- A figure that is too fast is explained by the work done, like one that is too slow. In L1a a change inside a row of fixed size took 0.2 µs: nothing was laid out, because a style change stopped at the node and never reached the parent (a defect since S6b, which a test expected).
- A script that turns pictures or traces into figures is checked against every app and every condition it reads before a figure is read from it. When one app or one size stands out, suspect the script first. In T4, windows cut from the first inked column moved the end stems of 24 px lines by 0.3 px (only for the grayscale apps), and windows moved one by one onto their stems drifted onto the next stem where LCD stems touch (PR #173).
- A scratch build changed by a script is checked for the change before it is measured. In T5 a `sed` meant to switch the font did not match, `grep` printed nothing, and the run measured the old font; the figures showed it (PR #219's retrospective).
- A difference that stays when a stage of one's own is turned off rules out only that stage, not the other side's. T4 wrote that the stem difference from Flutter was not a coverage correction because turning XylemUI's off did not change it; Flutter's own correction was the cause (PR #196).
- A first-time cost is measured at a size that no process has drawn as it is drawn, not as it is asked for. DirectWrite rounds a fractional size to whole pixels when it runs a font's hints: in T8, 28.433 px after 27.771 px (both 28 px) took 1.7 µs the first time, where a new size took 85 to 104 µs (PR #204).
- A picture the owner judges by eye comes from a window that is DPI aware. HelloWindow is not: at 125 % Windows stretches it, and bilevel text looked smoothed in the owner's screenshot; the text scene of `XylemUI.E2E`, which is DPI aware, showed it (PR #204).
- A first-time cost is measured once in a fresh process, with the caches that the OS keeps across processes in mind. Rounds repeated in one process measure the warm cost: in T4 the first rounds read 0.9 to 2.0 µs a glyph, while a glyph that no process had drawn at that size took 78 to 103 µs; DirectWrite shares a file font's glyphs at a size with other processes (PR #173).
- A second run of a path that keeps the last item it made (a rasterizer's last analysis or drawing) puts another item between the two runs. In T6 a measurement that put the wrong item between them read the cache: 1 µs for a second emoji (PR #192).
- A gain inside the spread is reverted, not shipped with other changes. A change tried for speed and not kept is reported with its measurement, so that it is not tried again.
- A design that names a technique for its speed is measured before the design PR says so. A prototype in a scratch project is enough.
- A measurement that a design or an Issue plans states what it expects before it runs, the worst case and the practical range, so that the result can be judged. T4 planned to measure the atlas with four glyph positions and gave no estimate; the review asked for it (worst 4 times, 1 to 2 times for the Japanese screen of S8b, PR #158).
- Named baselines: one per milestone, recorded with the commit and the command. A measurement quoted in an ADR, a comment, or a PR is recorded once and quoted from there.
- Do not add SIMD, caching, pooling, or unsafe code for speed unless a measurement shows the path is the bottleneck.
- A cache measures with every caller that reads it, on a change of one item in a large set, not only on a full rebuild. In S6b a one-entry layout cache per node was read by a flex container's measuring run and then by its layout run with another key; the two evicted each other, every clean child was computed again, and a one-node change cost as much as laying out 10,000 nodes (1,594 µs; 21.1 µs with one entry per kind of run).
